t  under as it doesn't seem to fit any of  the model predictions i just spent all  that time talking to you about well  let's take a closer look at what these  graphs are actually showing  so let's start with the graph on the  right because it's probably easier to  understand so what we can see here is  actually a negative relationship whereby  at higher activity energy expenditures  there's a lower basal energy expenditure  and so this actually fits in line with  the model of energy compensation the  idea that with increased activity  there's a reduction in basal energy  expenditure to compensate to allocate  that energy budget to recover from this  increased physical activity but if we  then look at the graph on the left we  see that there's still a linear  relationship between basal energy  expenditure and total energy expenditure  which means it doesn't 100  fit the energy compensation model and so  that linear relationship is what we saw  in the additive model and well it's also  seen in the performance model but the  key difference to notice is that it's  more about the strength of this  correlation between bee and tee that's  actually important because in the data  sets they see that the correlation has a  value of 0.723 so the slope is weaker  than the relationship seen in the  additive model and so if the correlation  equals 1 it assumes that there's  no link between active and basal  components of energy expenditure but the  fact that it's less than one suggests  that there's a considerable degree of  compensation occurring  between the active and basal components  and so we know that from looking at the  graph from the right  but the fact is it's not a hundred  percent compensation the compensation is  around 27.7  and so i tried to draw out what this  model predicts based on those stacks  that we saw earlier and another way of  thinking about it is that if you do a  workout and it says you burnt 300  calories so you decide to eat 300  calories of foods i